/-! # Normal-cone equations at a scheme near point

Source: Hironaka, Certain numerical characters of singularities (1970),
basic assumptions (2.1)--(2.2), Theorem III, section 13 and Proposition 21(i)--(ii),
pp.155--156, 168, 181 and 187; Dietel (2014), 9.1.8 and 9.2.1--9.2.2, pp.108--109
(blueprint: lem-per-scheme-near-point-order-generators).

The normal-cone residue fibre uses the symmetric tensor order k tensor_A G.
Its grading is specified by the spans of its canonical initial classes. The
Rees-to-fibre map is specified on every monomial, so the projective point is
identified with the original blowup point. No reducedness hypothesis is used.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertSamuel

/-- The homogeneous kernel of a minimal degree-one presentation of the normal-cone
fibre at a near point is generated by positive-degree forms whose localized order
is at least their degree. The equality is an ideal span, so it includes the empty
family when the kernel is zero.

Source: Hironaka (1970), Theorem III, p.156, section 13, p.168,
Proposition 21(i)--(ii), p.181, and the proof of Theorem IV, p.187;
Dietel (2014), 9.1.8 and 9.2.1--9.2.2, pp.108--109
(blueprint: lem-per-scheme-near-point-order-generators).

Proof (the node's nine steps):
1. Express R as a prime localization of a finite-type F-algebra. A polynomial
   presentation gives a regular local ambient ring T surjecting onto R, with
   the same residue field. Use lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-ring-field-regular-ring
   and lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-quotient-kernel-parameters to choose parameters
   (x_0,...,x_r,y_1,...,y_s) with P=(x_0,...,x_r) the inverse image of I.
   The prefix is nonempty: I=0 would make the Rees Proj empty.
2. Apply lem-trf-normal-flat-double-initial-ideal. Normal flatness identifies the
   tangent ideal E(T,a) with C k[X,Y], where C is the kernel of the specified
   ambient normal-fibre presentation B=k[X_0,...,X_r] onto S.
3. The chart cover prop-nagata-affine-blowup-chart-cover puts y in R[I/x_0].
   By lem-trf-parameter-blowup-algebra-regular the ambient chart
   T[t]/(x_0 t_i-x_i) is regular and its closed fibre is k[t]. Localizing its
   surjection onto R[I/x_0] gives T' onto R_y, with the correct maximal ideals
   and residue fields (def-nagata-blowup-chart-ring and ordinary localization).
4. Apply lem-per-permissible-blowup-standard-degrees to the raw near equality.
   It gives nuStar(T',a')=nuStar(T,a), without assuming R reduced.
5. Apply lem-per-permissible-adapted-standard-basis to obtain homogeneous forms
   phi_i in B of positive degrees d_i that generate E(T,a), whose
   dehomogenizations lie in p^d_i k[t]_p.
6. Evaluate Y=0 in E(T,a)=C k[X,Y]. This shows that the same phi_i generate C.
7. Since X_0 is outside the point prime q_B, write B[1/X_0]=k[t][X_0,1/X_0].
   The local fraction map k[t]_p to B_(q_B) sends p into its maximal ideal.
   The identity phi_i=X_0^d_i phi_i(1,t) gives phi_i/1 in that ideal's d_i-th power.
8. Compare the ambient presentation with the given minimal one Q onto S.
   Lift a basis of S_1 and append a basis V of the degree-one kernel. The
   resulting linear substitution identifies B=Q[V], C=(V)+JB and
   q_B=pi^(-1)(q), where pi sets V=0. Thus H_i=pi(phi_i) generate J and
   each nonzero H_i is homogeneous of positive degree d_i.
9. The induced local map B_(q_B) to Q_q preserves maximal ideals and their
   powers. Therefore every H_i has the required order. Discard zero forms.
   The ideal spanned by all qualifying forms contains these generators and
   is contained in J, hence equals J, also when J=0. -/
theorem scheme_near_point_order_generators
    {F : Type v} [Field F] {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    [IsLocalRing R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [Algebra F R] [Algebra.EssFiniteType F R]
    (I : Ideal R) [I.IsPrime] (hI : I ≤ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)
    [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ I)]
    (hcomponents : ∀ p ∈ minimalPrimes R, ¬ I ≤ p)
    [Module.Flat (R ⧸ I) (Ideal.associatedGraded I)]
    (𝒮 : ℕ → Submodule (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) (Ideal.normalConeFibre I hI))
    [GradedAlgebra 𝒮]
    (h𝒮 : ∀ n : ℕ, 𝒮 n = Submodule.span (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)
      (Set.range (Ideal.normalConeFibreClass I hI n)))
    (φ : reesGrading I →+*ᵍ 𝒮)
    (hφ : ∀ (n : ℕ) (a : (I ^ n : Ideal R)),
      φ (reesMonomial I a.val a.property) = Ideal.normalConeFibreClass I hI n a)
    (hirr : HomogeneousIdeal.irrelevant 𝒮 ≤ (HomogeneousIdeal.irrelevant (reesGrading I)).map φ)
    (y : AffineBlowup I) (hy : AffineBlowup.toSpec I y = IsLocalRing.closedPoint R)
    (t : Proj 𝒮) (ht : Proj.map φ hirr t = y)
    (δ : ℕ)
    (hδ : @Algebra.trdeg (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) ((Proj 𝒮).residueField t)
      _ _ ((Proj.toSpecOfAlgebra 𝒮).residueFieldAlgebraMap t).toAlgebra = (δ : Cardinal))
    (hnear : (summation^[δ]) (localFunction (AffineBlowup I) y) =
      fun n : ℕ => (Module.length R (R ⧸ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R ^ (n + 1))).toNat)
    (e : ℕ)
    (α : MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) →ₐ[IsLocalRing.ResidueField R]
      Ideal.normalConeFibre I hI)
    (hα : Function.Surjective α)
    (hgraded : ∀ (n : ℕ) (f : MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)),
      f.IsHomogeneous n → α f ∈ 𝒮 n)
    (hdegreeOne : Function.Bijective
      (Set.MapsTo.restrict α {f | f.IsHomogeneous 1} (𝒮 1 : Set _)
        (fun f hf => hgraded 1 f hf)))
    (q : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))) [q.IsPrime]
    (hq : q = Ideal.comap α.toRingHom t.asHomogeneousIdeal.toIdeal) :
    RingHom.ker α.toRingHom = Ideal.span
      {H : MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) |
        H ∈ RingHom.ker α.toRingHom ∧ ∃ d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ H.IsHomogeneous d ∧
          algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))
            (Localization.AtPrime q) H ∈
              (q.map (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))
                (Localization.AtPrime q))) ^ d} := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertSamuel
